## DESCRIPTION
## Write a linear system as a matrix equation.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## KEYWORDS('lines')

## DBsubject('WeBWorK')
## DBchapter('Linears and Linear Function')
## DBsection('Problem')
## Date('September 5, 2008')
## Author('Peter Staab')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')

# updated 2024.01.05

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGmatrixmacros.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Matrix")->noreduce('(-x)+y', '(-x)-y');

$zerov = Vector(0, 0, 0);
do {
    $A = Matrix([ map { [ map { random(-6, 6) } (1 .. 3) ] } (1 .. 3) ]);
    } until $A->row(1) != $zerov
    && $A->row(2) != $zerov
    && $A->row(3) != $zerov
    && Vector($A->column(1)) != $zerov
    && Vector($A->column(2)) != $zerov
    && Vector($A->column(3)) != $zerov;

$b = ColumnVector(map { random(-6, 6) } (1 .. 3));
$x = ColumnVector(Compute('<x,y,z>'));

@lines = map {
    Formula($A->element($_, 1) . 'x+'
            . $A->element($_, 2) . 'y+'
            . $A->element($_, 3)
            . 'z')->reduce;
} (1 .. 3);

BEGIN_PGML
Consider the linear system: 

[``` \begin{aligned}
[$lines[0]] & = [$b->{data}[0]] \\
[$lines[1]] & = [$b->{data}[1]] \\
[$lines[2]] & = [$b->{data}[2]] \\
\end{aligned}
```]

The linear system can be written as the matrix equation [`AX=B`] if

[`A=`] [_]*{$A}

[`X=`] [_]*{$x}

[`B=`] [_]*{$b}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
For this, the matrix [`A`] is the matrix of coefficients of the terms [`x, y`] and [`z`] respectively of the three equations.  This is 
[``` A= [$A]```]
The vector [`X`] is the vector of unknowns or 
[``` X = [$x] ```]
The vector [`B`] is the vector of the right hand side of the equations or 
[``` B = [$b]```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
